 "asked_at": "2026-10-16T21:32+08:00",
 "answer": "I can't pull a live reading, and the search results I got were snapshots from different times and sources. Some were clearly old. Here is what they showed:\n\n- **Weather Underground (Penang):** AQI 68, \"Moderate\", with PM2.5 as the main pollutant. It's based on Copernicus satellite and model data, not a ground sensor.\n- **AccuWeather (Penang International Airport):** AQI 67. The page labels this \"Poor\", though 67 is normally in the Moderate band.\n- **IQAir (Bukit Mertajam):** US AQI 58, \"Moderate\", with PM2.5 at about 12.9 µg/m³. The page was stamped 13 October.\n- **IQAir (George Town):** US AQI 125, \"Unhealthy for sensitive groups\". The page was stamped 16 October and is likely outdated.\n- **IQAir (Batu Feringgi):** US AQI 159, \"Unhealthy\". The page was stamped 9 October and is likely outdated.\n\nOverall, Penang usually sits in the Moderate range (about 50–100) when conditions are normal. It can climb into the unhealthy range during haze episodes, which are often linked to regional smoke or transboundary haze. The readings above disagree, partly because they come from different stations and models and were taken at different times.\n\nFor the current number, check one of these:\n- IQAir or AirVisual for George Town or Penang\n- The Malaysian Department of Environment's Air Pollutant Index (APIMS) portal, which is the official Malaysian source\n- The MyAQI or MyIPU apps\n\nIf the AQI is above 100, sensitive groups such as people with asthma, children and older adults should limit prolonged outdoor exertion.",
